Now I have all the code and the extended catalog. Here's the full academic audit.

---

## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · **.NET 10** · **Integration suite** (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

All 19 smells from the testsmells.org taxonomy were evaluated. Integration-test calibration was applied: Mystery Guest and Eager Test are downgraded for a class explicitly marked as integration scope.

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4** (of 8)   |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### ❶ Conditional Test Logic — **High**

**Smell:** `if`/`else` in test body routes execution to different assertions; one branch may never run, silently hiding a gap.

**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 101-108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Risk:** If `loaded` is never a `PremiumUser`, the discount-rate assertion never executes, giving false confidence. If the return type changes, the test silently switches branches.

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;

    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

---

#### ❷ Sleepy Test — **High**

**Smell:** `Thread.Sleep` introduces a hard 3-second wall-clock wait, making the suite slow and non-deterministic.

**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);
```

**Risk:** If the notification is slower than 3 s the test flakes; if it's faster, you waste 3 s on every run. This remains a smell even for integration tests — delays should be event-driven.

**Fix — poll with a timeout instead:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var sent = await WaitUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(sent, "Notification was not sent within the timeout.");
}

private static async Task<bool> WaitUntilAsync(Func<bool> condition, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;
    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (condition()) return true;
        await Task.Delay(50);
    }
    return false;
}
```

---

#### ❸ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — **High**

**Smell:** Test exercises production code but contains zero assertion calls. MSTest reports it as "passed" even if every row is corrupt.

**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113-119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
}
```

**Calibration note:** The name implies a "does not throw" intent, so the absence of assertions *may* be deliberate. Even so, verifying outcome strengthens the test considerably.

**Fix — assert the observable effect:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

---

#### ❹ Assertion Roulette — **Medium**

**Smell:** Multiple assertions with no explanation messages. When one fails, the runner reports only a line number — the developer must open the file to understand what went wrong.

**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35-38) — 4 consecutive assertions, none with a message parameter.

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Fix — add message parameters:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should be persisted.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should be persisted.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive.");
```

---

### Smells Not Detected (15 of 19 — Clean)

| Smell | Status | Notes |
|-------|--------|-------|
| Mystery Guest | ✅ Clean | In-memory SQLite is hermetic; `[TestCategory("Integration")]` makes DB use expected |
| Eager Test | ✅ Clean | Multi-method calls are normal for integration scope; no test exceeds 3 distinct production calls |
| Magic Number Test | ✅ Clean | `3` (line 76) and `10` (line 132) are self-documenting — items just inserted |
| Sensitive Equality | ✅ Clean | No `.ToString()` comparisons |
| Exception Handling | ✅ Clean | No `try`/`catch` blocks |
| General Fixture | ✅ Clean | Both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by all 8 tests |
| Ignored/Disabled Test | ✅ Clean | No `[Ignore]` attributes |
| Duplicate Assert | ✅ Clean | No duplicated assertion statements |
| Lazy Test | ✅ Clean | Tests share `Insert` for setup but verify distinct behaviors |
| Constructor Initialization | ✅ Clean | Uses `[TestInitialize]` correctly |
| Default Test | ✅ Clean | Class has a meaningful domain name |
| Redundant Print | ✅ Clean | No `Console.WriteLine` calls |
| Redundant Assertion | ✅ Clean | No tautological assertions |
| Resource Optimism | ✅ Clean | In-memory DB; no external file/service assumptions |
| Empty Test | ✅ Clean | All methods contain executable statements |

### Well-Written Tests Worth Highlighting

- **`DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase`** — clean arrange/act/assert, single concern, clear negative assertion (`Assert.IsNull`).
- **`InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity`** — properly awaits concurrent work, then asserts the aggregate result.
- **`ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted`** — good use of `CollectionAssert` alongside count verification.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Impact |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` after bulk insert | Eliminates false-pass risk (quick fix) |
| 2 | **Conditional Test Logic** | Split into two focused tests | Guarantees both branches are always exercised |
| 3 | **Sleepy Test** | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll loop | Removes 3 s of dead time + flakiness |
| 4 | **Assertion Roulette** | Add message strings to assertions | Improves failure diagnostics (cosmetic) |

The suite is **50% clean** (4 of 8 tests smell-free), with solid fundamentals — proper setup/cleanup lifecycle, hermetic in-memory DB, and well-scoped integration tests. The four findings above are straightforward to fix and would bring the suite to full academic compliance.
